<Article h1={"12-week dead hang program."} subtitle={"Three blocks, three sessions a week, reviews at weeks 4, 8 and 12. For a comfortable 30 seconds or longer. No promised hold time."} crumbs={[{"name":"Training plans","href":"/training-programs/"},{"name":"12 weeks"}]} updated={"Updated November 2, 2026"} title="12-Week Dead Hang Program: Schedule, Log & Review" description="A free 12-week dead hang program for a comfortable 30 second hold: three-block schedule table, 3 sessions a week, review points and a local training log." schema={`<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"12-Week Dead Hang Program: Schedule, Log & Review","description":"A free 12-week dead hang program for a comfortable 30 second hold: three-block schedule table, 3 sessions a week, review points and a local training log.","dateModified":"2026-11-02","mainEntityOfPage":"https://deadhangs.com/12-week-program/","author":{"@id":"https://deadhangs.com/#organization"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://deadhangs.com/#organization"}}</script><script type="application/ld+json">${faqLd}</script>`}>
<div class="article-body">
<div class="callout"><h2 id="glance" style="margin-top:0">At a glance</h2><ol class="stop-steps"><li><strong>Who it suits:</strong> a comfortable hold of 30 seconds or longer. Under that, use the <a href="/8-week-program/">8-week program</a> or the <a href="/4-week-program/">4-week program</a>.</li><li><strong>Schedule:</strong> 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days, 3 sets, 60 to 90 seconds of rest.</li><li><strong>Structure:</strong> three four-week blocks with a review at weeks 4, 8 and 12.</li><li><strong>Options:</strong> added load or one-arm work, each with its own prerequisite.</li></ol><p>These doses are common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p></div>
<h2>What is the 12-week dead hang program?</h2>
<p>The 12-week dead hang program is a three-block plan for people who hold a comfortable 30 seconds or longer. Each four-week block ends with a review, so you can keep a useful setup or change one variable. It adds load or one-arm work as options and does not promise a particular hold time.</p>
<p>For one session rather than a multi-week plan, see the <a href="/deadhang-guide/#workout-plan">dead hang workout plan</a> in the guide.</p>
<h2>12-week dead hang program schedule</h2>
<p>Use a securely fitted bar and a stable way to step down. Choose an assisted or unassisted hold you can control without pain and keep that variation consistent when comparing results. If you have an injury or concerns about the movement, seek individual guidance.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="12-week dead hang program schedule" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Weeks</th><th scope="col">Focus</th><th scope="col">Sessions per week</th><th scope="col">Sets and holds</th><th scope="col">Rest between sets</th><th scope="col">What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows.map(r=><tr><td><strong>{r[0]}</strong></td><td>{r[1]}</td><td>{r[2]}</td><td>{r[3]}</td><td>{r[4]}</td><td>{r[5]}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<p>The table is example guidance. It is common training practice and not a tested prescription, so adjust it from your own baseline.</p>
<h2 id="session-structure">How do you plan sessions around the rest of your training?</h2>
<p>Choose repeatable practice slots that fit your existing routine. Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days and add a session only if you stay pain free. Account for other grip work such as climbing or pulling exercises when deciding whether another hanging session is manageable.</p>
<ol><li>Write a starting setup for block one: bar, grip, support, hold interval, rest and sets.</li><li>Keep that setup stable enough to collect comparable sessions. Record what you actually complete.</li><li>Review at weeks four and eight. Keep a useful setup or change one variable for the next block.</li><li>Compare the last block with the first, using the same bar, grip and assistance. Export the record at week twelve before starting a new planning window.</li></ol>
<p><strong>Example decision:</strong> you complete more of your planned sessions in block two while your best hold stays at 30 seconds. Your consistency has improved even though your recorded maximum has not. A longer planning window does not require adding weight or moving to a one-arm hang.</p>
<h2>When can you add weight or one-arm work?</h2>
<p>Add weight only after a comfortable, pain-free bodyweight hang of about 45 seconds. Start one-arm progressions after a controlled two-arm hang of about 60 seconds, which is a conservative guide and not a tested threshold. The <a href="/weighted-hang-guide/">weighted dead hang guide</a> and the <a href="/one-arm-deadhang/">one-arm dead hang guide</a> set out each build-up.</p>
<h2>How do you track the 12-week program?</h2>
<p>The training log on this site tracks the 12-week program on your own device. Enter your own hang duration, rest duration and number of sets, then save the date, assistance, best actual hold and number of sets. Compare supported hangs with supported hangs and unassisted hangs with unassisted hangs.</p>
<a class="dh-btn" href="/training-log/">Open the timer &amp; log</a>
<h2>When should you change the plan?</h2>
<p>Change the 12-week plan when a hold is painful or when you cannot step down under control. Stop a painful hold and end a set before your control goes. A missed session does not require making up extra sets. Repeating a block is an option, and longer holds are not the only reason to keep a record.</p>
<h2>How do you review the 12 weeks?</h2>
<p>Review the program by comparing your current hold with an earlier hold made under the same conditions. That comparison with your own record is the number that matters. The <a href="/dead-hang-calculator/">personal calculator</a> shows the difference without inventing a population percentile.</p>
<p>The printable session planner on the <a href="/training-programs/">training programs page</a> suits you if you prefer paper. Dead hangs are one part of a wider routine; the <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/exercise/physical-activity-guidelines-for-adults-aged-19-to-64/">NHS activity guidance</a> covers broader strength and aerobic activity.</p>
